patches/ -- out-of-tree patches this TWRP build expects
=======================================================

These patches do NOT belong in the recovery repo (bootable/recovery), but in
OTHER AOSP repos. They live here so a rebuilder finds them alongside the
recovery repo and can optionally apply them. The recovery build itself
ignores this folder (no Android.mk/Android.bp -- pure distribution).


0001-adbd-bu-socketpair.patch
-----------------------------
Target repo : system/core
              (The recovery adbd is built from system/core/adb -- Android.bp
               `recovery_available: true` -> out/.../recovery/root/system/bin/adbd;
               NOT packages/modules/adb.)
File        : adb/daemon/services.cpp
Base        : pristine   md5 d51fbc1bf2fb07bd7afcf70cc326f33d
Result      : patched    md5 b91397ec395f58fe305825e181529c49

What:
  bu (adb backup/restore) gets a real socketpair from the recovery adbd instead
  of the forced PTY downgrade of the 4-arg StartSubprocess overload. A new
  static helper StartBuSubprocess() (SubprocessType::kRaw + Protocol::kNone +
  make_pty_raw=false); the two call sites `backup:`/`restore:` use it.
  __ANDROID__-guarded (mirrors the call sites -> no -Wunused-function in the
  host variant of services.cpp).

Why:
  Without the patch, `adb backup`/`adb restore` freezes SPORADICALLY and
  completely: the kernel tty layer (flip buffer + flush_to_ldisc workqueue +
  n_tty throttle wakeups) is race-prone under a sustained binary bulk stream --
  a lost wakeup leaves bu (n_tty_read), adbd (EPOLLOUT) and the host
  (A_OKAY) stuck forever. The only documented PTY purpose (SIGHUP kill
  for processes without their own read/write, e.g. screenrecord) does not apply
  to bu: bu pumps continuously and finishes even on EOF/EPIPE (SIGPIPE=SIG_IGN +
  fread-short hardening in twrpback.cpp).

OPTIONAL -- the patch is a convenience, not a must:
  WITHOUT it, this TWRP deliberately refuses a direct `adb backup`/`adb restore`
  (isatty refuse, otherwise freeze-prone) and you use the manifest-pure path:
      adb shell adbbu start          # starts the short-lived twadbd mode
      adb backup --twrp ... > x.ab   # then runs over twadbd's socketpair
  WITH the patch, a direct `adb backup` works without this extra step.

Apply (IN THE TARGET REPO system/core, NOT in the recovery repo):
      cd system/core
      git apply  /path/to/bootable/recovery/patches/0001-adbd-bu-socketpair.patch
  or:
      cd system/core
      patch -p1 < /path/to/bootable/recovery/patches/0001-adbd-bu-socketpair.patch
  Both paths transform the pristine file exactly into md5 b91397ec (verified).

Then rebuild adbd/recovery. NOTE:
  - Ramdisk-stale trap: after the rebuild, verify that
    out/.../recovery/root/system/bin/adbd was actually re-staged
    (strings/md5), otherwise the old adbd keeps running in the ramdisk.
  - `repo sync`/checkout in the system/core tree overwrites the patch -> then
    apply it again.

Revert:
      cd system/core && git apply -R <patch>     (or patch -R -p1 < <patch>)
  The host command (`adb backup --twrp ...`) and the .ab file format are
  completely unaffected by BOTH patch and revert -- only the adbd<->bu pipe
  changes.
